WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.560
Tyler: Welcome everyone.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.000
Tyler: I'm Tyler Yahn.

3
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.822
Nikola: And I'm Nikola Grcevski.

4
00:00:04.822 --> 00:00:10.080
Nikola: Today we're following a slow customer checkout, from the first symptom to a fix.

5
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.874
Tyler: The application completes the request, but the customer is waiting.

6
00:00:13.874 --> 00:00:18.391
Tyler: We'll follow that request across the system, add language detail, and change the

7
00:00:18.391 --> 00:00:19.086
Tyler: code responsible.

8
00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:23.603
Tyler: Then we'll use the OpenTelemetry Injector and a small demo operator to share

9
00:00:23.603 --> 00:00:25.340
Tyler: that setup across the application.

10
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.003
Nikola: One customer problem, then a way for each of the teams in the organization to

11
00:00:31.003 --> 00:00:31.715
Nikola: investigate theirs.

12
00:00:31.715 --> 00:00:33.140
Nikola: Let's open the system.

13
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.923
Tyler: Here is our checkout application: eighteen services, with PostgreSQL and Redis.

14
00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:41.711
Tyler: The Gateway hands the request to Checkout, which calls Inventory and four

15
00:00:41.711 --> 00:00:42.343
Tyler: commerce branches.

16
00:00:42.343 --> 00:00:47.394
Tyler: The map shows what can talk to what; the duration tells us the whole request is

17
00:00:47.394 --> 00:00:47.710
Tyler: slow.

18
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.210
Nikola: Every team can have a different definition of healthy.

19
00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.390
Nikola: Following the request gives us one shared starting point.

20
00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:58.474
Tyler: First, OpenTelemetry eBPF Instrumentation, or OBI.

21
00:00:58.474 --> 00:01:02.650
Tyler: It's a zero-code way to observe running Linux applications.

22
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:07.754
Tyler: It uses eBPF to watch application and network activity, then exports

23
00:01:07.754 --> 00:01:09.610
Tyler: OpenTelemetry traces and metrics.

24
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:14.120
Nikola: We're getting a starting point across the application without asking each team

25
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.913
Nikola: to add a library or restart its service.

26
00:01:16.913 --> 00:01:20.404
Nikola: That's a lot of visibility before the first instrumentation ticket.

27
00:01:20.404 --> 00:01:23.197
Nikola: We showed this idea last year in Atlanta.

28
00:01:23.197 --> 00:01:25.990
Nikola: Now OBI is version one and generally available.

29
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.961
Tyler: And this is more than a service map.

30
00:01:28.961 --> 00:01:32.276
Tyler: OBI follows supported calls across service boundaries, including our HTTP,

31
00:01:32.276 --> 00:01:33.601
Tyler: PostgreSQL, and Redis traffic.

32
00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:37.910
Tyler: Let's deploy it on the nodes and let it discover the running applications.

33
00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.022
Nikola: One deployment.

34
00:01:39.022 --> 00:01:40.605
Nikola: Eighteen services to investigate.

35
00:01:40.605 --> 00:01:44.167
Nikola: Much better odds than guessing which team to call.

36
00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:45.750
Nikola: Let's open a request.

37
00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.042
Nikola: Look what that one OBI deployment gave us.

38
00:01:54.042 --> 00:01:58.590
Nikola: This is the slow request behind the opening number, followed across the

39
00:01:58.590 --> 00:01:58.969
Nikola: application.

40
00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:04.275
Nikola: Here are the HTTP calls and the PostgreSQL price query, before we've added a

41
00:02:04.275 --> 00:02:06.170
Nikola: language agent to any service.

42
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:09.614
Tyler: Different languages, one request we can follow.

43
00:02:09.614 --> 00:02:13.861
Tyler: We've gone from a customer waiting for seconds to a specific set of service

44
00:02:13.861 --> 00:02:14.772
Tyler: calls and durations.

45
00:02:14.772 --> 00:02:17.806
Tyler: That's a huge head start for such a small setup.

46
00:02:17.806 --> 00:02:19.626
Tyler: The customer is still waiting, though.

47
00:02:19.626 --> 00:02:21.750
Tyler: Let's find the part we can change.

48
00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.004
Tyler: Watch the plain topology turn into an observed request path.

49
00:02:26.004 --> 00:02:30.139
Tyler: We can now ask which calls happened, which services handled them, and where the

50
00:02:30.139 --> 00:02:30.730
Tyler: time went.

51
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.737
Nikola: Here we're following the Cart branch through Catalog, Recommendations, and

52
00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:35.106
Nikola: Promotions.

53
00:02:35.106 --> 00:02:39.530
Nikola: This is one branch of the same request moving through the system.

54
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.130
Tyler: Start with the waterfall.

55
00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.970
Tyler: Gateway's long span includes its wait for Checkout, and Checkout's span includes

56
00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:46.930
Tyler: its downstream work.

57
00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.130
Tyler: The longest parent span doesn't tell us what to fix.

58
00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.954
Nikola: Look at this failed connection from Checkout instead.

59
00:02:52.954 --> 00:02:57.024
Nikola: It consumes about two seconds before the successful product lookup and the other

60
00:02:57.024 --> 00:02:57.650
Nikola: service calls.

61
00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.301
Tyler: Now we have somewhere specific to investigate: why is Checkout spending time on

62
00:03:03.301 --> 00:03:04.572
Tyler: a connection that fails?

63
00:03:04.572 --> 00:03:09.021
Tyler: OBI has exposed the failed attempt; language detail can connect it to the method

64
00:03:09.021 --> 00:03:10.610
Tyler: and fallback in our code.

65
00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:14.210
Nikola: Let's open Checkout and follow that connection.

66
00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.090
Tyler: We have source access here.

67
00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.338
Tyler: Pricing runs before Inventory.

68
00:03:18.338 --> 00:03:23.018
Tyler: We'll use the Java agent to time that method and expose the library calls inside

69
00:03:23.018 --> 00:03:23.330
Tyler: it.

70
00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.702
Nikola: This is OpenTelemetry's zero-code instrumentation.

71
00:03:25.702 --> 00:03:31.037
Nikola: The agent supplies the instrumentation; we still need to make it available, add

72
00:03:31.037 --> 00:03:34.730
Nikola: the startup setting, and configure where its data goes.

73
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.446
Tyler: Here's the deployment change.

74
00:03:39.446 --> 00:03:41.540
Tyler: The application image stays the same.

75
00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.076
Tyler: We provide the agent, set the service identity and exporter, and enable the

76
00:03:46.076 --> 00:03:46.774
Tyler: pricing-method span.

77
00:03:46.774 --> 00:03:48.170
Tyler: Then we roll Checkout.

78
00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:52.399
Nikola: Zero code is useful, but hasn't made the release calendar disappear.

79
00:03:52.399 --> 00:03:57.375
Nikola: Someone still needs the runtime knowledge, the settings, and a way to deploy the

80
00:03:57.375 --> 00:03:57.730
Nikola: change.

81
00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:02.083
Tyler: Here's a fresh request with the Java agent.

82
00:04:02.083 --> 00:04:02.841
Tyler: Open PricingEngine.calculate.

83
00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:07.012
Tyler: The long interval contains a failed HTTP connection, then a successful

84
00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:07.770
Tyler: product-price lookup.

85
00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.092
Nikola: Open the failed HTTP span.

86
00:04:11.092 --> 00:04:13.495
Nikola: Its error type is Java's SocketTimeoutException.

87
00:04:13.495 --> 00:04:16.299
Nikola: The exception event says, Connect timed out.

88
00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:18.301
Nikola: That is a connection timeout.

89
00:04:18.301 --> 00:04:22.707
Nikola: The product-price lookup after it succeeds, so Checkout completes, but the

90
00:04:22.707 --> 00:04:25.510
Nikola: customer pays for the failed attempt first.

91
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.388
Tyler: OBI got us to Checkout.

92
00:04:27.388 --> 00:04:30.817
Tyler: The language agent exposed the failed attempt and the working fallback.

93
00:04:30.817 --> 00:04:33.310
Tyler: Now we have a code change to investigate.

94
00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:35.422
Tyler: There it is.

95
00:04:35.422 --> 00:04:38.127
Tyler: Pricing still calls a legacy backend that no longer exists.

96
00:04:38.127 --> 00:04:41.914
Tyler: The connection times out after two seconds, then we read the price from the

97
00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:42.726
Tyler: current product store.

98
00:04:42.726 --> 00:04:44.890
Tyler: The fix is to use that store directly.

99
00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:46.738
Nikola: Instrumentation didn't fix the code.

100
00:04:46.738 --> 00:04:48.267
Nikola: It enabled us to act.

101
00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:52.852
Nikola: If we can collaborate with the owning team, we can bring a pull request instead

102
00:04:52.852 --> 00:04:55.603
Nikola: of a vague report that their service is slow.

103
00:04:55.603 --> 00:05:00.188
Nikola: Here is the customer impact, this is the trace showing the timeout, and now we

104
00:05:00.188 --> 00:05:03.550
Nikola: have a change that removes it while keeping the price lookup.

105
00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.902
Tyler: We're deploying that application change now.

106
00:05:05.902 --> 00:05:08.950
Tyler: The Java agent and exporter settings stay the same.

107
00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:24.973
Nikola: The rollout is ready.

108
00:05:24.973 --> 00:05:28.030
Nikola: Let's send another customer request and look at its trace.

109
00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:31.196
Tyler: Here is the new request's waterfall.

110
00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:32.540
Tyler: Expand Checkout and PricingEngine.calculate.

111
00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.556
Tyler: The product-price query is still here.

112
00:05:34.556 --> 00:05:39.260
Tyler: The failed legacy call is gone, and the rest of the request starts sooner.

113
00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.700
Nikola: Follow it through Inventory, the commerce branches, and the order write.

114
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.573
Nikola: We kept the useful work.

115
00:05:45.573 --> 00:05:50.442
Nikola: The customer gets a faster checkout because we're no longer waiting for a

116
00:05:50.442 --> 00:05:51.940
Nikola: backend that isn't there.

117
00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:56.790
Tyler: This is the payoff: a customer symptom, an observed cause, a code change, and a

118
00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.300
Tyler: fresh trace showing what changed.

119
00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.349
Tyler: Across matched requests, the median checkout went from about two point two

120
00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:05.530
Tyler: seconds to two hundred and twenty milliseconds.

121
00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:08.646
Tyler: Pricing dropped from about two seconds to under four milliseconds.

122
00:06:08.646 --> 00:06:12.697
Tyler: That supports the change we just followed in the waterfall: stop making the

123
00:06:12.697 --> 00:06:15.190
Tyler: customer wait for a backend that is gone.

124
00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.160
Nikola: That summary supports the trace we just saw.

125
00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.472
Nikola: It is a local demo comparison, not a production benchmark.

126
00:06:21.472 --> 00:06:26.110
Nikola: Next, how do we make the same diagnostic detail available to the other teams?

127
00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.807
Nikola: We've fixed this checkout.

128
00:06:28.807 --> 00:06:32.650
Nikola: But getting to that answer needed runtime knowledge, configuration, access, and

129
00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:33.349
Nikola: a deployment.

130
00:06:33.349 --> 00:06:37.890
Nikola: How do the other teams get this detail without repeating all that setup?

131
00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:43.340
Tyler: In this demo, thirteen more services use runtimes supported by the OTel

132
00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:43.768
Tyler: Injector.

133
00:06:43.768 --> 00:06:47.188
Tyler: The Go services will keep their OBI coverage.

134
00:06:47.188 --> 00:06:51.890
Tyler: Our next step is to share the setup across those thirteen.

135
00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:54.570
Nikola: And share the routine work around it.

136
00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:59.290
Nikola: Not every team has our access, our runtime knowledge, or an empty release queue.

137
00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:04.204
Tyler: To see what we're centralizing, here's the upstream Operator's opt-in

138
00:07:04.204 --> 00:07:04.564
Tyler: configuration.

139
00:07:04.564 --> 00:07:08.158
Tyler: Java and Python have different injection annotations, with an Instrumentation

140
00:07:08.158 --> 00:07:09.596
Tyler: resource supplying their settings.

141
00:07:09.596 --> 00:07:13.190
Tyler: You can put those annotations on workloads or a namespace.

142
00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.299
Nikola: That already saves work.

143
00:07:15.299 --> 00:07:20.665
Nikola: For this talk, we built RIO, the Runtime Instrumentation Operator, in OTelX.

144
00:07:20.665 --> 00:07:25.136
Nikola: It's a demo operator that combines central namespace selection, the

145
00:07:25.136 --> 00:07:27.372
Nikola: OpenTelemetry Injector, and managed rollouts.

146
00:07:27.372 --> 00:07:33.185
Nikola: We want to bring this capability upstream, whether in the existing Operator or

147
00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:34.080
Nikola: another form.

148
00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:37.210
Nikola: That path still needs design and agreement.

149
00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.978
Tyler: Here we install the demo controller once.

150
00:07:40.978 --> 00:07:43.076
Tyler: It builds on Jack Berg's prototype.

151
00:07:43.076 --> 00:07:45.873
Tyler: The controller selects workloads and manages their rollout.

152
00:07:45.873 --> 00:07:49.370
Tyler: The OpenTelemetry Injector handles supported runtimes and loads their agents.

153
00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:53.146
Nikola: The payload, exporter, and exclusions are shared settings.

154
00:07:53.146 --> 00:07:57.898
Nikola: Application owners don't each choose a language annotation and arrange a

155
00:07:57.898 --> 00:07:59.194
Nikola: separate instrumentation rollout.

156
00:07:59.194 --> 00:08:02.650
Nikola: Next comes the application choice: instrument this namespace.

157
00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.620
Nikola: Here is the selection rule: instrument demo-apps.

158
00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.891
Nikola: We select the application boundary once.

159
00:08:08.891 --> 00:08:13.813
Nikola: Unlike the language opt-in we just showed, this rule doesn't list languages or

160
00:08:13.813 --> 00:08:14.570
Nikola: individual services.

161
00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:21.697
Tyler: Apply that rule, and the controller rolls the selected workloads.

162
00:08:21.697 --> 00:08:24.742
Tyler: The Injector injects and supplies the OTel agents.

163
00:08:24.742 --> 00:08:28.930
Tyler: Compatible services deployed into the namespace later inherit the same policy.

164
00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.852
Nikola: Now all the supported services are lit up.

165
00:08:46.852 --> 00:08:51.213
Nikola: Checkout keeps its direct agent, thirteen more services gain language detail,

166
00:08:51.213 --> 00:08:53.988
Nikola: and the four Go services keep OBI.

167
00:08:53.988 --> 00:08:57.953
Nikola: The ongoing responsibility lives with the platform, instead of thirteen

168
00:08:57.953 --> 00:08:59.539
Nikola: application teams repeating it.

169
00:08:59.539 --> 00:09:01.521
Nikola: OBI is still running here.

170
00:09:01.521 --> 00:09:05.882
Nikola: It detects services already exporting traces through an OpenTelemetry SDK and

171
00:09:05.882 --> 00:09:07.072
Nikola: avoids duplicate tracing.

172
00:09:07.072 --> 00:09:10.640
Nikola: We didn't have to reconfigure OBI for every service.

173
00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:16.365
Tyler: Back in the application view, we're looking at a new request through the

174
00:09:16.365 --> 00:09:17.041
Tyler: instrumented fleet.

175
00:09:17.041 --> 00:09:21.436
Tyler: We still have the system view, and now we can open language-level observations

176
00:09:21.436 --> 00:09:22.450
Tyler: in more services.

177
00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:23.916
Nikola: Let's follow Catalog.

178
00:09:23.916 --> 00:09:27.737
Nikola: These are the Java agent's database spans from that service.

179
00:09:27.737 --> 00:09:33.086
Nikola: The same enrollment also covers the Node, Python, Ruby, and dot net services in

180
00:09:33.086 --> 00:09:33.850
Nikola: this application.

181
00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:38.257
Tyler: The namespace rule got us here without a separate instrumentation change from

182
00:09:38.257 --> 00:09:39.279
Tyler: each application team.

183
00:09:39.279 --> 00:09:40.642
Tyler: Checkout keeps its fix.

184
00:09:40.642 --> 00:09:45.069
Tyler: Now the other teams have the language detail ready for their next investigation

185
00:09:45.069 --> 00:09:45.410
Tyler: too.

186
00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:48.864
Tyler: Think about the direct setup we watched.

187
00:09:48.864 --> 00:09:51.912
Tyler: We knew the runtime, had access, and controlled the release.

188
00:09:51.912 --> 00:09:55.570
Tyler: How much happens before someone in a real organization can even begin?

189
00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.130
Nikola: Find an owner.

190
00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.370
Nikola: Explain the request.

191
00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:02.503
Nikola: A ticket, a meeting, access, review, and a release slot.

192
00:10:02.503 --> 00:10:05.810
Nikola: Then somebody switches away from their feature work.

193
00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.922
Tyler: One namespace choice can remove repeated enrollment changes and keep shared

194
00:10:09.922 --> 00:10:10.612
Tyler: settings consistent.

195
00:10:10.612 --> 00:10:13.370
Tyler: The saving is more than typing fewer commands.

196
00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.179
Nikola: It is fewer people arranging the same work.

197
00:10:16.179 --> 00:10:19.290
Nikola: Count their active effort separately from the time spent waiting.

198
00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.582
Tyler: And setup is only the first bill.

199
00:10:22.582 --> 00:10:23.709
Tyler: A new service arrives.

200
00:10:23.709 --> 00:10:25.118
Tyler: An agent needs an update.

201
00:10:25.118 --> 00:10:27.090
Tyler: A team needs to turn instrumentation off.

202
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.464
Nikola: Compatible new services inherit the namespace choice.

203
00:10:30.464 --> 00:10:33.081
Nikola: Updates reuse the shared rollout path.

204
00:10:33.081 --> 00:10:37.008
Nikola: Configuration, documentation, and recovery instructions have one place to

205
00:10:37.008 --> 00:10:37.444
Nikola: improve.

206
00:10:37.444 --> 00:10:42.679
Nikola: A runtime change doesn't automatically mean a new onboarding project: OBI can

207
00:10:42.679 --> 00:10:47.478
Nikola: keep the baseline for supported traffic, while language-level detail depends on

208
00:10:47.478 --> 00:10:48.350
Nikola: the runtime.

209
00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:51.130
Tyler: That can reduce drift and repeated troubleshooting.

210
00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:53.590
Tyler: But the platform does not maintain itself.

211
00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:54.382
Nikola: Exactly.

212
00:10:54.382 --> 00:10:57.686
Nikola: Compatibility, rollout, and recovery still need people.

213
00:10:57.686 --> 00:11:00.990
Nikola: Their time goes into the calculation too.

214
00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.890
Nikola: This is a sketch of adoption, not measured rollout data.

215
00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:09.130
Nikola: Team-by-team ownership advances in steps: one service, then another.

216
00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:13.450
Nikola: Busy teams create a long tail that may never reach full coverage.

217
00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:15.482
Tyler: And the finish line moves.

218
00:11:15.482 --> 00:11:16.509
Tyler: New services arrive.

219
00:11:16.509 --> 00:11:20.617
Tyler: Someone rewrites a service from Java to Python, and the old instrumentation

220
00:11:20.617 --> 00:11:21.986
Tyler: setup no longer fits.

221
00:11:21.986 --> 00:11:25.410
Tyler: Coverage can drop while other teams are still catching up.

222
00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:28.147
Nikola: Central policy makes coverage the default.

223
00:11:28.147 --> 00:11:32.980
Nikola: One rollout reaches the eligible services in scope, and new compatible workloads

224
00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:33.785
Nikola: inherit it.

225
00:11:33.785 --> 00:11:37.410
Nikola: The platform still tests and maintains that shared path.

226
00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:41.437
Tyler: Let's keep the cost comparison in this demo.

227
00:11:41.437 --> 00:11:44.821
Tyler: Eighteen services, thirteen of them still needing language instrumentation after

228
00:11:44.821 --> 00:11:45.836
Tyler: our first example.

229
00:11:45.836 --> 00:11:49.558
Tyler: These steps show setup and two years of monthly agent updates.

230
00:11:49.558 --> 00:11:51.250
Tyler: Each jump is another release.

231
00:11:51.570 --> 00:11:55.268
Nikola: With distributed ownership, each service owner arranges the work.

232
00:11:55.268 --> 00:12:00.610
Nikola: With centralized control, we set up the namespace once and share each update.

233
00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:05.130
Nikola: Both lines include upkeep; the centralized path still costs engineering time.

234
00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:09.269
Tyler: Over two years, that frees two hundred and forty-seven engineering hours.

235
00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:13.436
Tyler: At a hundred dollars an hour, that's twenty-four thousand seven hundred dollars.

236
00:12:13.436 --> 00:12:17.603
Tyler: From setup and monthly updates, in the same small application we've been

237
00:12:17.603 --> 00:12:17.950
Tyler: investigating.

238
00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:22.598
Nikola: These are assumed person-hours valued at a hundred dollars an hour, not a

239
00:12:22.598 --> 00:12:23.597
Nikola: measured cash saving.

240
00:12:23.597 --> 00:12:25.928
Nikola: Include your own compatibility and support costs.

241
00:12:25.928 --> 00:12:29.590
Nikola: We haven't priced the calendar time spent waiting for other teams.

242
00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.178
Tyler: We started with a customer waiting for checkout.

243
00:12:33.178 --> 00:12:34.608
Tyler: OBI showed the failed connection.

244
00:12:34.608 --> 00:12:37.468
Tyler: The Java agent connected it to pricing and its fallback.

245
00:12:37.468 --> 00:12:41.186
Tyler: We removed the stale backend attempt, then returned to the actual request trace

246
00:12:41.186 --> 00:12:42.330
Tyler: to see the change.

247
00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.554
Nikola: Then one namespace choice carried language instrumentation across the supported

248
00:12:46.554 --> 00:12:46.944
Nikola: services.

249
00:12:46.944 --> 00:12:52.020
Nikola: The next team can start with useful detail instead of starting another setup

250
00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:52.410
Nikola: project.

251
00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:54.341
Tyler: The customer gets a faster checkout.

252
00:12:54.341 --> 00:12:57.564
Tyler: The teams get a shared way to investigate the next slow request.

253
00:12:57.564 --> 00:13:00.250
Tyler: That is what the traces and the automation were for.

254
00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:02.382
Nikola: The demo shows the idea.

255
00:13:02.382 --> 00:13:07.094
Nikola: We want an upstream home for this workflow, with the design still open.

256
00:13:07.094 --> 00:13:08.181
Nikola: Find the wait.

257
00:13:08.181 --> 00:13:09.268
Nikola: Fix the cause.

258
00:13:09.268 --> 00:13:11.080
Nikola: Make the next investigation easier.

259
00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:12.530
Nikola: Let's take your questions.
